data: {"delta":{"thinking":"The user is asking me to generate a title for a conversation where the message is \"Reply with the single word: pong\".\n\nThis is a simple, minimal/conversational message. It's like a test or a playful command to see if I respond with \"pong\". Based on the rules:\n\n\"If the user message is short or conversational (e.g. \"hello\", \"lol\", \"what's up\", \"hey\"):\n  → create a title that reflects the user's tone or intent (such as Greeting, Quick check-in, Light chat, Intro message, etc.)\"\n\nThis seems like a quick test or check-in, possibly like a \"ping-pong\" test. The tone is playful/minimal.\n\nGood title options:\n- \"Ping pong test\" - 14 chars, captures the intent\n- \"Pong response test\" - 18 chars\n- \"Quick pong check\" - 15 chars\n- \"Ping test\" - 9 chars\n\nI think \"Ping pong test\" works best as it captures what's being asked - a simple ping-pong exchange.\n\nLet me verify it meets the requirements:\n- Single line: ✓\n- ≤50 characters: ✓ (14 chars)\n- Grammatically correct: ✓\n- No tool names: ✓\n- Reflects the tone/intent: ✓ (it's a quick test/check)\n- Not \"summarizing\" or \"generating\": ✓\n- Something meaningful: ✓\n\nActually, looking at the examples more carefully, they're focused on what the user WANTS TO DO. Here the user is just asking for a pong response - it's like a test or quick interaction. Maybe something like:\n\n- \"Pong response\" - 14 chars\n- \"Ping-pong exchange\" - 18 chars\n- \"Quick pong test\" - 14 chars\n\nI think \"Pong response\" or \"Quick pong test\" works. Let me go with \"Pong response\" as it's more direct and reflects what the conversation is about - they want a pong response.\n","type":"thinking_delta"},"index":0,"type":"content_block_delta"}

